  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThere is currently an upper trough axis across the Gulf coast with \ntwo areas of surface low pressure, one along the Florida panhandle \nand another just off shore from the GA/SC border. The trough and \nassociated surface lows will lift north and east through the \nremainder of the weekend and into the new work week. This will bring \ntwo periods of light and benign rain chances in the near term, \nthough, we will continue to see low RH values for a few more hours \nuntil surface dewpoints and RHs begin to notably recover around 22z. \n\nAs for the rain chances, the first period will come this evening and \ninto early Sunday afternoon. Some light radar returns can be noted \nacross our southwest North Carolina counties at the time of this \ndiscussion, but with RHs currently in the 20s to low 30s this is \nmore than likely virga. As low level saturation increases later \nthis evening and tonight, light rain chances will increase. Rain\namounts expected are between a few hundredths upwards of one-\nquarter of an inch in favored upslope locations in SW NC and TN\nmountains. This period of rain will generally favor areas east of\n75. \n\nLatest trends in hi-resolution guidance suggest we will then see a \nperiod of drier conditions late Sunday afternoon into the evening \nbefore a weak cold front brings additional rain chances Sunday night \ninto Monday. Due to the moisture starved nature of the front, any\nrain during this period will be rather benign and only amount to \na few hundredths or perhaps a tenth or two for a few isolated \nlocations.\n\nMonday night into Tuesday will feature cooler temperatures in a post-\nfrontal airmass and drier conditions. By the mid-week, an additional \ntrough is expected to swing across the deep south bringing a return \nof rain chances. Still some timing and amplitude discrepancies this \nfar out, but overall, this system looks to bring better chances for \nsome light rain relative to this weekend. If deterministic models \nchoose to trend more towards the more amplified GFS, we could even\nsee some breezy winds across the mountains and foothills. Mild \nand drier conditions look to finish the week off behind this \nsystem.",
